x86_64-linux  The C1 and D1 forms, and nothing else: every name this case is about - `_ZTS1PIiE`, `_ZTV3BoxIiLi3EE`, `_ZTSN2ns1RIiEE`, `_ZN6HolderIS_IiEEC1ES0_` and the rest - agrees with clang letter for letter. clang emits only the base-object C2 and D2 forms of a constructor and destructor defined *inside* their class on this target and calls those directly, where cxx1 emits the complete-object forms beside them; the bodies are identical for a class with no virtual base, and both are mergeable definitions, so the cost is object size. The same fact move-constructor.nonames, implicit-move.nonames and reference-temporary-lifetime.nonames already record, and it is why this file has no x86_64-windows or arm64-darwin line: both of those agree with clang name for name. The other cases answer it by defining the constructor out of line, which this one cannot - an out-of-line constructor of a class template is one of the four gaps CLAUDE.md lists as met while writing <type_traits>.
